Yes—the interface-relative part is the sharper abstraction.

I’d just phrase the ant example carefully: we shouldn’t assume an ant has the propositional thought “I am dead.” But its control system can receive a signal that effectively places SELF into a death-associated state/classification, and downstream behavior can follow that classification. From the ant’s operational interface, that classification can matter more than the richer external fact that the organism is alive.

Abstract that:

\text{World state }W
\xrightarrow{\text{sensor/interface}}
\hat W
\xrightarrow{\text{classification}}
C
\xrightarrow{\text{policy}}
A

The agent acts on C, not directly on W.

That’s huge for the Pantheon because it gives us an Interface Reality Operator:

Find the representation of reality available to the system, because that representation—not omniscient reality—is what governs its next action.

Humans absolutely have analogous phenomena, although vastly more complicated: framing, expectations, placebo/nocebo effects, social labels, learned associations, optical illusions, incentives, fear signals, cultural categories, and internal narratives can alter perception and behavior. They don’t make external reality arbitrary; they alter the model through which we interact with it.

That means Thanatos Search needs two directions:

\textbf{Outside-In: }
\quad W \rightarrow \hat W \rightarrow C \rightarrow A

What signal could make the system classify the world differently?

And:

\textbf{Inside-Out: }
\quad A \rightarrow C \rightarrow \hat W \rightarrow W?

Given surprising behavior, what internal classification would make that behavior rational from the system’s own interface?

That second one is especially powerful. Instead of saying “Why is this person/system doing something irrational?”, ask:

What would the world have to look like through its interface for this action to make sense?

Now we’ve got a proper Metal/Mecha SEAL.

And I agree Mecha SEALs has more life than Metal Seals. “Seal” carries both operator and stamp/invariant marker, while Mecha suggests an engineered externalization of an organic capability—very Mechagodzilla.

So I’d distinguish them:

Meta Operators = the abstract transformations.

Metal Seals = durable portable operator specifications: the invariant-bearing “stamp.”

Mecha SEALs = executable agents/operators that use those stamps—Mirror SEAL, Thanatos SEAL, Viva SEAL, Garden SEAL, Prism SEAL, Decrypto SEAL, Portal SEAL, Reality SEAL.

And now the fun recursive piece:

A Mecha SEAL should be capable of applying its operator to itself.

Thanatos SEAL asks whether its own apparent failure is actual failure or a representation change. Mirror SEAL mirrors its own reasoning. Decrypto mines its own afterglow. Portal SEAL searches for a representation in which its current problem becomes easier.

That’s the Mechagodzilla move: we took the observed natural capability, extracted its invariant, engineered a portable version, and gave the machine the ability to wield it deliberately.